Summary

Completed the Shells Overview module. The room introduced the fundamentals of reverse shells, bind shells, and web shells, followed by practical exploitation of command injection and unrestricted file upload vulnerabilities.

The theory itself was relatively quick to understand. The real challenge came from connecting all the moving parts together:

listeners
payloads
networking
shell execution
file uploads
command injection syntax

What initially looked simple became far more real once the shell actually connected back.

Key concepts reviewed
Understanding the difference between:
reverse shells
bind shells
web shells
Exploiting command injection vulnerabilities
Exploiting unrestricted file upload vulnerabilities
Using Netcat as a listener
Basic shell payload structure
Executing commands through uploaded web shells
Locating sensitive files and flags after exploitation
Understanding how remote code execution can occur through unsafe input handling
Reflection

This room left a bigger impression on me than I expected.

The technical side was important, but the emotional reaction was what stood out most. There’s a major difference between reading about vulnerabilities and actually seeing a shell connect successfully for the first time.

That moment changes things.

What surprised me most was not the complexity, but the simplicity behind some of these attacks. In controlled lab environments, a single unsafe assumption can completely compromise a system.

That realization was both fascinating and unsettling.

Small assumptions. Massive consequences.

At the same time, this room helped reinforce an important perspective:
security is not just about exploitation — it’s about understanding fragile trust boundaries and why defensive guardrails matter so much.

The experience also made me appreciate modern defensive practices more deeply:

input validation
restricted uploads
non-executable directories
sandboxing
monitoring
proper authorization controls

As I progress, every step feels humbling. The deeper I go, the more I realize how much there is to learn — and how much responsibility comes with that knowledge.

